INT. SALT-MINE OBSERVATORY - NIGHT

A cavern hewn from white crystal, three kilometers under a desert. Great mirrors tilt on rails, collecting the dark. Salt crystals glitter in the lamplight like frozen stars.

CASSIA, 30s, an astronomer in a headlamp and thermal suit, crouches over a console. Across the cavern, DOMINIC, 50s, her supervisor, stands at a railing, staring into the largest mirror.

CASSIA
Dominic. Come see this.

DOMINIC
I'm busy.

CASSIA
It isn't noise.

DOMINIC
It's always noise.

CASSIA
It repeats every eleven seconds.

He turns. He walks toward her, slowly, and the walk becomes a stride. At the console, he leans over her shoulder.

DOMINIC
That's the same signal.

CASSIA
That's what I said.

DOMINIC
From the same place?

CASSIA
From inside the planet.

A long pause. In the mirror behind them, the dark ripples, as though it has caught its own breath.

DOMINIC
(very quiet)
Cassia, switch off the receivers.

CASSIA
Why?

DOMINIC
Because it's answering.

The console beeps. A new line of numbers scrolls across the screen, an eleven-second pulse, perfectly in time with her heartbeat.

CASSIA
(whisper)
Dominic.

DOMINIC
I know.

She lays her hand over the switch. He lays his hand over hers. Neither pulls it down.
